### Execute a transaction with EntityManager

Source: https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/blob/master/docs/docs/working-with-entity-manager/5-entity-manager-api.md

The `transaction` method runs multiple database requests in a single transaction. Use the provided manager instance for all operations within the transaction.

```typescript
await manager.transaction(async (manager) => {
    // NOTE: you must perform all database operations using the given manager instance
    // it's a special instance of EntityManager working with this transaction
    // and don't forget to await things here
})
```

--------------------------------

### Create transaction using DataSource

Source: https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/blob/master/docs/docs/transactions.mdx

Starts a transaction using the DataSource's `transaction` method. The callback receives a `transactionalEntityManager` that must be used for all operations within the transaction.

```typescript
await myDataSource.transaction(async (transactionalEntityManager) => {
    // execute queries using transactionalEntityManager
})
```

--------------------------------

### Pessimistic write locking with setLock

Source: https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/blob/master/docs/docs/query-builder/1-select-query-builder.md

Use setLock("pessimistic_write") to acquire an exclusive lock on the selected rows. Supported on all listed databases.

```typescript
const users = await dataSource
    .getRepository(User)
    .createQueryBuilder("user")
    .setLock("pessimistic_write")
    .getMany()
```

--------------------------------

### Update a photo using Repository

Source: https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/blob/master/docs/docs/getting-started.md

Loads a single photo by id using findOneBy, modifies its name property, and saves the change with save(). The update is persisted to the database after the save call.

```typescript
import { Photo } from "./entity/Photo"
import { AppDataSource } from "./index"

const photoRepository = AppDataSource.getRepository(Photo)
const photoToUpdate = await photoRepository.findOneBy({
    id: 1,
})
photoToUpdate.name = "Me, my friends and polar bears"
await photoRepository.save(photoToUpdate)
```

--------------------------------

### Use custom repository in transaction

Source: https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/blob/master/docs/docs/working-with-entity-manager/4-custom-repository.md

Uses a custom repository inside a transaction. You must use the manager instance provided by the transaction and its withRepository method, because global entity managers and repositories won't work in transactions.

```typescript
await dataSource.transaction(async (manager) => {
    // in transactions you MUST use manager instance provided by a transaction,
    // you cannot use global entity managers or repositories,
    // because this manager is exclusive and transactional

    const userRepository = manager.withRepository(UserRepository)
    await userRepository.createAndSave("Timber", "Saw")
    const timber = await userRepository.findByName("Timber", "Saw")
})
```
